    h1: "How Do You Design Foundations on Nashville's Karst Limestone?",
    answer: "Rock you can see is not always rock you can trust. Direct answer: foundations on Nashville's Ordovician limestone are designed around karst. The geotechnical engineer maps dissolution voids, pinnacle rock, and soil-filled solution features with borings, rock coring, and probe holes; the structural engineer then bears foundations on proven sound rock, most often with drilled shafts rock-socketed past the weathered zone. The project permits through the Metro Nashville Department of Codes and Building Safety, the single authority for Nashville's consolidated city-county government.\n\nNashville sits on the Ordovician limestone of the Central Basin, and slightly acidic groundwater has spent millions of years dissolving it along joints and bedding planes. The result is karst: voids below, pinnacle rock where sound stone rises in jagged spires between clay-filled troughs, and sinkholes where the surface drops into a hidden void. A spread footing placed on what looks like solid rock can bridge a void that later settles, and a footing on a clay-filled solution feature settles differently from its neighbor. The investigation therefore proves rock continuity, not just rock presence.\n\nThe standard investigation combines soil borings with rock coring to confirm rock quality, plus probe holes at each foundation location to confirm no void lurks beneath the bearing elevation. Geophysical methods such as ground-penetrating radar or electrical resistivity can screen larger sites for anomalies between borings, but they do not replace physical probing at the foundation. Where probes find voids, the remediation is filling the void with flowable fill or grout before the foundation bears, or extending the drilled shaft deeper to socket into sound rock below the feature.\n\nThe structural design treats each foundation as a custom bearing point. Drilled shafts are typically socketed several feet into competent rock, with the socket length set by the geotechnical engineer from core recovery and rock quality. Grade beams tie the shafts together so differential movement at one location cannot rack the frame. Special inspection during shaft drilling verifies rock bearing at the bottom of each shaft, because the rock surface mapped during design is a prediction and the excavation is the truth. Because Nashville and Davidson County are one consolidated government, Metro Codes reviews the whole project; a few miles outside the county line in Williamson, Rutherford, Sumner, or Wilson County, the same foundation follows that county's own building department, portal, and review timeline. The AHJ decides what is approved, so the engineer confirms the jurisdiction's geotechnical submittal expectations before the investigation starts.",
