    answer: "Knoxville's river is managed but not tamed. Direct answer: buildings near the Tennessee River in Knoxville are designed from FEMA flood insurance rate maps that set the base flood elevation, with finished floors elevated above it, flood-resistant materials below, and floodproofing or elevation certificates documenting compliance. The Tennessee Valley Authority's dams moderate river flows but do not eliminate floodplain regulation, and projects permit through the City of Knoxville's Plans Review and Inspections office or Knox County Codes Enforcement and Engineering depending on which side of the city line the parcel sits on.\n\nThe TVA system, including Fort Loudoun Dam just downstream of Knoxville and the chain of reservoirs across the valley, regulates the Tennessee River for flood control, navigation, and power. That regulation flattens the everyday river but the floodplain maps still govern construction, because the maps reflect the residual risk: extreme rainfall overwhelming tributaries, dam operations during major events, and the backwater that pushes up creeks like First Creek and Second Creek when the main river runs high. Engineers design to the mapped floodplain, not to the river's normal level, and the AHJ enforces the maps as adopted.\n\nThe flood design itself follows the national playbook with local enforcement. The surveyor establishes the base flood elevation at the site from the effective FIRM panel, and the architect sets the lowest finished floor above it, with the local freeboard requirement added where the jurisdiction requires elevation beyond the federal minimum. Below the elevated floor, the design uses flood-resistant materials, breakaway walls or properly vented enclosures in the flood zone, and mechanical and electrical equipment placed above the flood level. An elevation certificate prepared by a licensed surveyor documents the as-built elevations for the permit file and for flood insurance rating.\n\nWhere the building cannot be elevated, dry floodproofing of nonresidential structures is the alternative: reinforced walls, sealed openings, and closures rated for hydrostatic and hydrodynamic loads, all designed and certified by the engineer. Wet floodproofing, letting water in and out of sacrificial enclosures, suits parking and storage areas. Stormwater adds a second water problem independent of the river: Knoxville and Knox County each enforce stormwater management for runoff quantity and quality, so the civil engineer designs detention and water-quality treatment even on sites far above the floodplain. Jurisdiction matters here too: city projects follow Knoxville's stormwater and floodplain ordinances, county projects follow Knox County's, and the two are similar but not identical. The AHJ decides what is approved in both.",
    directAnswer: "Knoxville flood design starts with FEMA maps that set the base flood elevation, then elevates finished floors above it with flood-resistant construction below and elevation certificates documenting compliance. TVA dams moderate river flows but do not remove floodplain regulation. City projects permit through Knoxville Plans Review and Inspections; county projects go through Knox County Codes Enforcement.",
    topic: "Tennessee Valley Mid-South Engineering",
    serviceHref: "/services/civil/",
    sections: [
      {
        heading: "FEMA Maps, Base Flood Elevation, and Freeboard",
        body: "The flood insurance rate map is the legal geography of flood risk. Its zones tell the engineer what rules apply: high-risk zones where elevation or floodproofing is mandatory, and shaded or unshaded moderate zones where lenders may still require coverage. The base flood elevation printed on the map is the water surface of the one-percent-annual-chance flood, and every flood design decision hangs from that number.\n\nFreeboard, the extra elevation above the base flood elevation that a jurisdiction requires, is where local rules bite. It buys margin against map uncertainty, future development upstream, and events bigger than the mapped flood, and it usually pays for itself in lower flood insurance premiums. The surveyor's elevation certificate is the proof document: it records the building's lowest floor elevation against the base flood elevation so the AHJ and the insurer can verify compliance without re-surveying the project.",
      },
      {
        heading: "TVA Operations, Tributaries, and Stormwater",
        body: "TVA's reservoir operations shape the river Knoxville sees day to day, holding back flood storage in the wet season and releasing it in a controlled way. But tributary flooding does not wait for the main river: intense storms can flood First Creek, Second Creek, and the smaller streams through Knoxville's neighborhoods while the Tennessee River itself stays in its banks. The floodplain maps capture both, and the engineer checks the site against the tributary panels as carefully as the river panels.\n\nStormwater regulation is the parallel track. Knoxville's and Knox County's stormwater programs require new development to manage peak runoff and water quality, typically with detention basins, underground systems, or green infrastructure sized to local design storms. A site outside the mapped floodplain still needs a full stormwater design, and a site inside it needs both floodplain compliance and stormwater compliance coordinated so the detention design does not conflict with the floodproofing. The civil engineer sequences these early because they set grading, which sets everything else.",
      },
    ],
