        answer: "Dry floodproofing seals a nonresidential building so floodwater cannot enter, using reinforced walls, sealed openings, and closures designed for water pressure. Wet floodproofing allows water to enter and exit sacrificial areas like parking garages through vents, equalizing pressure so walls are not damaged. Residential buildings generally must be elevated rather than dry-floodproofed.",
